The extent of a reaction, named $x$--and often $\xi$--is the extent to which the reaction proceeds until reaching equilibrium. This concept, introduced around 100 years ago by belgian scientist Th\'eophile de Donder, is also known as the progress of a reaction, advancement of the reaction, or the conversion. Some reactions will proceed so that most of the reactants are decomposed into products, and hence the extent will be large, whereas others will proceed to a lesser extent so that fewer reactants become products. $x$ is a single value for a reaction depending on the equilibrium constant value, temperature and the starting concentrations of reactants and products in the mixture. When using the ICE tabular method the extent of the reaction is represented as $x$. 